  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low storm chances continue today with higher humidity \nand hotter temperatures. More storms Friday as the front sags south.\n\nIt's another mostly dry day ahead with just a few spotty\nshowers, and perhaps a rumble of thunder toward the I-64\ncorridor. Skies are largely devoid of clouds with temperatures\nalready pushing into the upper 70s to low 80s. Still monitoring\nthe upper level ridge nearby over the central and southeastern \nU.S along with a nearby stalled front draped from the VA \nTidewater westward into the TN/Ohio River Valleys. Pieces of \nenergy will continue to traverse this boundary within west to \nnorthwest flow today through Friday. Confidence in timing and \nthunderstorm coverage is low for today given the heightened \nridge with increasing chances Friday as the ridge flattens and \nfront moves through. \n\nThe earlier mentioned front will remain a focus for spotty\nshowers this afternoon. The signal in high-resolution models is\npretty weak, so it could easily remain dry today. This boundary\nwill gradually lift back toward the north late tonight into \nFriday with another wave of low pressure set to push through. \nThis will lead to increased chances for showers and \nthunderstorms across the region with a focus, especially along \nand south of I-66/US-50. Thunderstorm activity will likely occur\nduring the peak heating period with locally damaging winds, \nfrequent lightning, and isolated instances of flash flooding as \nthe primary concerns. SPC continues to highlight areas along and\nsouth of US-33 in VA with a Marginal Risk (Level 1 out of 5) \nfor severe weather. This aligns with machine learning/ AI \nguidance which suggests 5 to 15 percent probs for severe weather\nFriday afternoon and evening. A lot of this will be dependent \nupon cloud cover and any interaction from potential MCSs diving \nacross the central and lower Ohio River Valley and into far \nsouthern WV/southwest VA. Some guidance remains aggressive like \nthe NAMnest while the HRRR, ARW, and NSSL remain more reasonable\nwith coverage/magnitude. No washouts are expected although \nchances will increase during the peak heating period, especially\nsouth and west of the I-95 metro areas. \n\nHigh pressure builds from the north Friday night shunting the front \nfurther south of the region. This will allow for drier conditions to \nprevail along with a brief break in the humidity. Highs today will \npush into the mid 80s and low 90s with heat index values in the mid \nto upper 90s east of the Alleghenies. Overnight lows will fall back \ninto the upper 60s and low 70s with highs Friday into the 80s. \n\nAmple high pressure builds to the north of the region on Saturday \nshunting the front into the Carolinas. An onshore component to the \nwind could keep some additional cloud cover around with partly \ncloudy skies expected. Humidity will briefly drop with highs in the \nlow to mid 80s (upper 80s in the city centers and 70s over the \nmountains).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Showers and thunderstorms possible between Sunday \nand  Monday before becoming drier into early next week.\n\nBeginning Sunday, an upper-level perturbation forms over the \nCanadian Rockies and tracks eastward, with a stemming frontal \nboundary tracking across the East Coast late Sunday and into Monday. \nThis boundary will likely bring chances for scattered showers and \nthunderstorms here in the Mid-Atlantic, although model guidance has \nbeen somewhat marginal on the severe potential for this event. As of \nthe 18z ensemble runs, several outputs have the highest likelihood \nfor severe weather just south of the area towards southern VA and \nthe Carolinas, while the GFS ensemble runs still have a 15%-30% \nprobability for severe weather centered within the region. Should \nany thunderstorms become severe, the most likely hazards will likely \nbe gusty winds as well as locally heavy rainfall. As drought \nconditions continue to improve within the region, flooding potential \nbecomes more of a concern with any heavy rainfall-producing \nthunderstorms that materialize. \n\nOtherwise, temperatures remain near-normal for mid-August, with \nhumid conditions as well as temperatures ranging in the 80s and \nlower-90s at times near the metros. High pressure moves in around \nthe Ohio River Valley, shifting surface winds coming out of the NW. \nOnce that frontal boundary passes offshore by Tuesday, it could \nbring in some much needed relief to the humid conditions expected \nthis weekend.",
